%S A398920 0,0,1,3,8,14,25,38,55,77,104,133,170,214,258,313,372,440,511,592,674,
%T A398920 776,873,985,1094,1230,1356,1505,1646,1814,1971,2159,2335,2547,2729,
%U A398920 2960,3167,3421,3651,3917,4158,4458,4717,5036,5319,5663,5958,6331,6642,7028
%N A398920 Number of solutions of the Diophantine inequality a*b+b*c+c*d <= n, with a,b,c >= 1.
%C A398920 a(n) is also the number of triangles of area at most n/2 whose vertices are at (-x1,-y1), (0,y2) and (x2,0), where x1, x2, y1 and y2 are positive integers.
%C A398920 The vertices of these triangles form the pattern 132. This pattern specifies the required relative order of the y-coordinates of the vertices when read from left to right. Thus, the leftmost vertex must be the lowest, and the other two vertices must form a descending edge.
%C A398920 a(n) is also the number of lattice quadrilaterals of area at most n/2 whose vertices form the pattern [32]11. (Here, the brackets denote points with the same x-coordinate.)
%C A398920 a(n) is also the number of L-shaped lattice hexagons of area at most n (whose vertices form the pattern [13][32][21] and whose edges visit the vertices in the listed order).
%C A398920 a(n) is also the number of integer partitions of at most n with exactly two part sizes.
%F A398920 Conjecture: a(n) = n^2*log(n)^2/4 + O(n^2*log(n)).
%e A398920 a(4) = 3: The triangle with vertices {(-1,-1), (0,1), (1,0)} has area 3/2; the two triangles with vertices {(-1,-2), (0,1), (1,0)} and {(-2,-1), (0,1), (1,0)} have area 2.
%t A398920 a[n_] := Sum[Floor[(n-(a+b)c)/a], {a, (n-1)/2}, {b, n-2a}, {c, (n-a)/(a+b)}]; Array[a, 50]
%Y A398920 Partial sums of A002133.
%K A398920 nonn,new
%O A398920 1,4
